  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nAfter a brief bout of light snow showers over portions of the\narea this morning, the afternoon hours have been quiet with\ngradually decreasing cloud cover. This is happening on the\nbackside of a vigorous/potent upper low that is still digging\nSEward through the MO River Valley. Subsidence behind this wave\nwill likely keep our weather quiet for at least the next couple\nof days. Thanksgiving Day will be on the chilly side with highs\nonly in the 40s...but wind will be light and there will be\nplenty of sunshine. The cold start to the morning, combined with\nvery light winds and clear skies, could lead to patchy areas of\nfog in the morning. HRRR runs are showing this most prominently\nin valley locations from around Lexington to Ord. Felt the\npotential was too limited/isolated to include in the HWO at this\ntime, but will pass along concerns/potential to the evening\nshift for further evaluation.\n\nBlack Friday will be a bit warmer in the upper 40s and 50s\nbehind a weak trough that will provide some weak, westerly\ndownsloping flow. Will likely have some mid to high clouds to\ncontend with, but expect the day to remain dry.\n\nOur next precipitation chance will come with a progressive\nshortwave trough on Saturday. Models have been fairly consistent\nwith the timing and track of this system and PoPs have risen\naccordingly into the likely to definite categories. Obviously,\nwith us being in the 50s the day before, this system isn't going\nto have much cold air to work with, at least initially. Thus,\nexpect the majority of precip to fall as cold rain in the upper\n30s and 40s. However, both diurnal and at least modest dynamic\ncooling could allow for a mix or even outright changeover to \nwet snow for some areas - particularly W/NW of the Tri-Cities \nSaturday eve/night. Latest deterministic EC seems like a decent\nidea of this potential. This could support a quick dusting to\nperhaps half inch of wet snow accumulations where it does change\nover, but not expecting much more than that as even EC temps\nremain above freezing under the snow band through the eve hrs.\n\nThe rest of the forecast is fairly quiet and seasonable. May\neven make a run back into the 50s towards the middle of next\nweek.",
